# sideman — the organ cabinet A/B, as an honest one.
#
# The Side Man had no speaker: it fed the organ's amplifier and cabinet, so the
# cart pins that stage from outboard.h (the WARM console EQ into the asymmetric
# IRON stage) and C switches the whole thing out.
#
# TWO things this script exists to get right, both of which quietly ruin the
# comparison:
#
#  1. PARK THE PLATE FIRST (V). With a reverb send in circuit, every stage's A/B
#     reconverges only after the tail carries the difference away, over a second
#     at the default size (analog-outboard-chain.md §4's corollary). So the plate
#     goes out at frame 60, well before the first switch.
#  2. LEAVE A WHOLE BAR EACH WAY. At the default 124 BPM in 4/4 a bar is ~116
#     frames, so the switches sit 120 frames apart: one full bar with the
#     cabinet, one without, one with it back.
#
# Measured off exactly this shape (two renders, sample-diffed): bit-identical up
# to the first switch, and reconvergence to bit-exact 0.304s after the switch
# back in. The stages null exactly; re-engaging one waits for the chain's own
# memory, most likely the EQ's 80 Hz band.
down 60  V
up   62  V
down 240 C
up   242 C
down 360 C
up   362 C
down 480 C
up   482 C
